    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ea7780fc2fa2c3047414d456032356a0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1918,
    "chunk_text": "5.(1) Any person who abets the commission of an offenceunder this Act shall be guilty of an offence and shall onconviction\u2014\n\n(a)if the offence abetted is committed in consequenceof the abetment, be liable to the same punishmentas is provided for the offence; and\n\n(i)where a maximum fine or term ofimprisonment is provided for, to a fine notexceeding one fourth of the maximum fineprovided for the offence or to imprisonmentof either description for a term not exceedingone fourth of the maximum term provided forthe offence, or to both such fine andimprisonment; and\n\n(ii)in cases where the maximum fine orimprisonment is not provided for, to a finenot exceeding two hundred and fifty thousandrupees or to imprisonment of eitherdescription for a term not exceeding five yearsor to both such fine and imprisonment.\n\nAttempts tocommit offences.\n\nAbetment of anoffence.\n\n6Payment Devices Frauds Act, No. 30 of 2006\n\n(2) The term \u201cabet\u201d shall have the same meaning as insections 100 and 101 of the Penal Code (Chapter 19) and theprovisions of sections 101A, 103, 104, 105, 106 and 107 ofthe Penal Code shall mutatis mutandis apply in relation tothe abetment of any offence under this Act.\n\n6.(1) Any person who conspires to commit an offenceunder this Act shall be guilty of an offence and shall, onconviction after trial before the High Court be liable to bepunished with the punishment prescribed for abetting thecommission of that offence.\n\n(2) The term \u201cconspire\u201d shall have the same meaning as insection 113A (2) of the Penal Code (Chapter 19) and theprovisions of that section shall mutatis mutandis apply inrelation to a conspiracy to commit an offence under this Act.\n\nPART II\n\nINVESTIGATION\n\n7.Except as otherwise provided in this Act, all offencesunder this Act shall be investigated, tried or otherwise dealtwith in accordance with the provisions of the Code of CriminalProcedure Act, No. 15 of 1979."
